#
# .gitattributes controls three things for this repository:
#
#   1. Line-ending normalization -- what gets stored in the object database
#      (always LF) versus what gets checked out into the working tree.
#   2. Diff behaviour -- which files Git should treat as opaque blobs so that
#      generated artifacts don't drown real changes in review.
#   3. GitHub Linguist hints -- language stats, generated/vendored flags, and
#      what counts as documentation.
#
# Rules apply top-to-bottom and later rules win for the same attribute, so
# keep the sections in order: defaults first, exceptions after.
#
# Reference: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes

###############################################################################
# Core text handling / line endings
###############################################################################

# Default policy: let Git auto-detect text vs. binary, and store text as LF.
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree even on Windows checkouts, so the
# bytes a contributor sees match what CI sees. Per-type rules below either
# reaffirm this or carve out deliberate CRLF exceptions.
* text=auto eol=lf

